$DIR/main.p:1:1: //go:build is a Go directive; write //pu:build
    Plutonium does not read //go: comments, so this one would do nothing. Its
    directives start with //pu:, and //go:build is //pu:build there.
    Go ignores //go: comments it does not know, which makes a typo easy to miss.
    Plutonium directives change what the program means, so a comment that looks
    like one is never ignored.
    Use the //pu: directive:
        //pu:build linux
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2604" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:8:1: //go:generate is a Go directive, and Plutonium has no equivalent; delete it
    Plutonium does not read //go: comments, so this one would do nothing, and no
    //pu: directive does what //go:generate does in Go. The directives are:
    blocking, build, export, extern, fpu, inline, interrupt, nobounds, noescape,
    noinline, noreturn, nosimd, panic, percpu, resultholds, scheduler, section,
    stack.
    Go ignores //go: comments it does not know, which makes a typo easy to miss.
    Plutonium directives change what the program means, so a comment that looks
    like one is never ignored.
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2604" for a longer explanation)

